Admission and close use the same Mutex. If admission wins, its accepted connection is appended before close marks closed and captures that owner. Close drains the captured list and closes those sockets outside the state lock. If close wins, admission cannot append: it closes the late accepted socket outside the lock and returns false; the single-accept peers return, and the header peer breaks its accept loop. There is no interval where a successfully admitted socket can miss both ownership paths. Closing the listener also releases the no-accept case, including shutdown before the worker has reached accept. Repeated close is idempotent at this owner level; later calls cannot admit owners or detach a new owner list.

Completion uses CheckedContinuation registrations under the same state Mutex. Worker completion captures and removes all waiters under that lock, then resumes them outside it. A waiter arriving after completion resumes through the immediate path. Multiple waiters can observe the same completion; none replace another waiter. No DispatchGroup, semaphore, condition wait, polling sleep or blocking completion wait remains in this file. The completion observation covers the peer script and its socket cleanup; it does not claim physical OS-thread exit, result-memory deallocation, a hard time bound or a generic DNS cancellation bound.

